<p>The shop's product cache has been running for a year. Product pages read <Term kind="redis">Redis</Term> about 1,000 times a second, about nine reads in ten are <Term kind="hit" mark>hits</Term>, and the <Term kind="database">database</Term> behind it answers the hundred or so <Term kind="miss" mark>misses</Term> a second without strain. The team that built the cache has moved on, and you are now the person who gets paged when it misbehaves.</p>

<p>The questions you will be asked are these: what happens to the site if Redis goes away, and what should the application do while it is gone? Redis came back empty after maintenance; how do we put it back into service without taking the database down? The hit ratio dropped last night; why? How much memory does it need for next year's catalogue? Who is allowed to connect to it? And should we run it ourselves, buy it as a service, or move to Valkey?</p>

<p>This article answers them in that order. By the end you will be able to tell whether your system survives losing its cache, design what the application does meanwhile, refill an empty cache safely, choose alerts from Redis's counters, size its memory, lock it down, and judge a managed service. Figures 1, 2, 3 and 5 are models of the shop; Figure 4 replays counters I measured on a real Redis 8.10.3 server on my laptop.</p>

<h2 id="dependency">Latency cache or capacity cache</h2>

<p>As <ArticleRef slug="">the introduction</ArticleRef> showed, losing the cache multiplies the database's load. With a hit ratio of <em>h</em>, the database normally sees the fraction 1 − <em>h</em> of all reads, and straight after the cache is lost it sees all of them: 1 ÷ (1 − <em>h</em>) times its normal load, ten times at a 90% hit ratio. An operator turns that around: given the database you have, how much traffic can the system carry, with the cache and without it?</p>

<p>Call the database's capacity <em>C</em> queries a second and the read traffic <em>R</em> reads a second. With a warm cache the database receives the misses, so it copes as long as</p>

<p class="formula"><em>R</em> × (1 − <em>h</em>) ≤ <em>C</em>, that is, <em>R</em> ≤ <em>C</em> ÷ (1 − <em>h</em>)</p>

<p>With an empty cache every read is a query, so it copes only while <em>R</em> ≤ <em>C</em>. Those two limits divide all possible traffic into three ranges. Below <em>C</em> the system works with or without the cache. Above <em>C</em> ÷ (1 − <em>h</em>) it fails even when the cache is warm. In between, it works only while the cache is warm. Figure 1 draws the ranges for a database that can answer 300 queries a second, the example Bronson and colleagues use in their paper on metastable failures: failures that keep themselves going after their cause has gone.</p>

<CapacityFigure number={1} />

<p>At a 90% hit ratio, the shop's 1,000 reads a second sit well inside the middle range: the warm cache leaves the database 100 queries a second, a third of its capacity, but an empty cache would hand it more than three times what it can answer. Now move the slider to 99% and notice that only the solid line moves. The warm limit jumps to 30,000 reads a second, while the dashed line still crosses capacity at 300. A better cache lets the system carry more traffic while the cache is there, and does nothing for the moment it is lost. At 50% the middle range shrinks to 300 to 600 reads a second, and the shop's traffic overloads the database even with a warm cache.</p>

<p>The Google SRE book names the two situations. A <strong>latency cache</strong> makes responses faster, but the service could sustain its expected load with the cache empty. A <strong>capacity cache</strong> is one without which the service cannot sustain its expected load. Bronson and colleagues call the middle range the <strong>vulnerable state</strong>: the system is healthy, may stay healthy for months, and is one trigger away from a failure it cannot leave on its own. Many systems run there on purpose, because it is far cheaper than a database sized for every read.</p>

<p>Nobody has to decide to enter that range. A cache added to make pages faster starts as a latency cache; traffic grows, the cache absorbs the growth, and one day the traffic is past <em>C</em>. The SRE book says caches added for performance "wind up being hard dependencies." Bronson and colleagues describe a version that looks like good engineering: a better eviction policy lowers the database's load, someone reclaims the spare database capacity, and the system can no longer recover from losing the cache.</p>

<p>Neither kind is wrong; a capacity cache is often the only affordable design. What matters is knowing which you have, because the next sections are about what a capacity cache needs. If your system sits in the left-hand range of Figure 1 and will stay there, much of the next three sections is insurance you may decide not to buy.</p>

<h2 id="testing">Failure testing</h2>

<p>The arithmetic needs numbers you may not know. <em>R</em> and <em>h</em> come from your metrics, at peak rather than on average. <em>C</em> is harder: how many of these queries your database can answer a second before its latency climbs, which only a load test against a copy tells you reliably.</p>

<p>The surer test is to take the cache away on purpose. Start in a test environment, then in production for a small share of traffic, such as one application instance at a quiet hour. Watch the database's load, latency and errors, and the application's. The drill also exercises code that otherwise never runs; in the SRE book's words, "the code path you never use is the code path that (often) doesn't work."</p>

<p>How you take the cache away matters, because a dead Redis and a stuck one look different to the application. On my laptop, with Redis stopped, a <code>GET</code> on a new connection failed in under a millisecond: the operating system refused the connection. <code>CLIENT PAUSE 3000 ALL</code> makes a running server stop answering every client for three seconds while keeping their connections open, as a hung process would; a <code>GET</code> with a 50 ms timeout then failed after 50 ms, and one with no practical timeout waited the full three seconds. A dead host or a network that drops packets is a third case: nothing refuses the connection, so only the connect timeout ends the wait, and a firewall rule that drops the port's traffic rehearses it. A drill that only stops Redis tests the easy case. Lowering <code>maxmemory</code> on a test server rehearses a cache that is too small, and flushing one rehearses a cold start.</p>

<h2 id="degrading">While the cache is down</h2>

<p>When Redis becomes unavailable, every request that would have been a hit needs another answer. The application has three jobs: notice quickly, avoid burying the database, and decide what the requests it cannot serve get.</p>

<h3 id="fail-fast">Failing fast: timeouts and circuit breakers</h3>

<p><ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining" hash="timeouts">Redis clients</ArticleRef> covered the first part: connect and command timeouts of a few milliseconds, and retries that are few, spaced out and budgeted. That still leaves every request paying the timeout while Redis is down, and trying to reconnect.</p>

<p>A <strong>circuit breaker</strong> removes that cost. It is a wrapper around calls to a dependency that counts failures. While calls succeed it is <em>closed</em> and passes them through. When failures pass a threshold, say half of the calls in the last ten seconds, it <em>opens</em>: calls fail at once without touching the network, and for a cache that means an immediate miss. After a while it lets a single trial call through (<em>half-open</em>); if that succeeds it closes, and if not it stays open for another period. For a cache, a breaker also stops thousands of application threads from reconnecting to a Redis that has just restarted and is still loading, and its state is a switch the rest of the application can read: "the cache is down, use the degraded path."</p>

<h3 id="limit">Bounding the work sent to the database</h3>

<p>Failing fast moves the problem to the database: every read is now a miss. Figure 2 shows what decides whether that becomes an outage. It is the shop, scaled down to 20,000 products so that your browser can simulate it. Redis becomes unavailable at 10 s and comes back at 40 s with its data intact, as it would when a replica is promoted after Sentinel's default detection time of 30 seconds. Its database runs eight queries at a time. The choice changes one thing: how many queries the database may hold at once, waiting or running.</p>

<OutageFigure number={2} />

<p>Start with no limit, and watch the chart and the database's wait. In the outage's first second, 41% of requests are still answered within the one-second timeout; after that, none are. A thousand queries a second arrive at a database that can answer 300, the queue grows by 700 a second, and every answer comes back after the request that asked for it has given up.</p>

<p>Press Skip ahead until Redis is back at 40 s, then keep skipping. The 90% of reads that hit the cache are answered at once, but each miss joins the back of a queue that is now 70 seconds long, behind queries whose requests failed long ago. So roughly one request in ten keeps failing until about 144 s, more than a minute and a half after the cache was healthy again, and the database finishes about 40,000 queries nobody was waiting for. In an incident review this looks baffling: the cache is back, the hit ratio is normal, and pages are still failing.</p>

<p>Now choose 60. While Redis is down, the database answers about 300 requests a second, each within a fifth of a second, and the other 700 fail at once. That is 30% of requests served, against the faint no-limit line near zero, and the moment Redis returns everything is served again, because there is no backlog. The limit adds no capacity; it gives the capacity there is to requests still waiting for an answer.</p>

<p><ArticleRef slug="caching-patterns" hash="read-through">Caching patterns</ArticleRef> showed the same mechanism from the other side, in its Figure 4: when Redis comes back empty, a database working through abandoned queries never answers in time, so nothing refills the cache and the system stays stuck, the metastable failure Bronson and colleagues describe. The limit is one way out. Here the cache came back full, and the backlog alone prolonged the outage.</p>

<p>How large should the limit be? Try the other values. At 4, half the database's eight workers are never used: only 14% of requests are served during the outage, and even before it a burst of misses is sometimes refused. At 300 and 600 the queue the limit allows is one and two seconds long, so admitted queries miss their deadline again: at 300 only about one request in six is served, against nearly one in three at 60, and at 600 almost none are. Both outages still end when Redis returns, because the queue is short.</p>

<p>So the limit has two bounds. It must be at least the number of queries the database can usefully run at once, or the database idles while requests are refused. And it must be at most the database's capacity times the time a query may spend waiting and running, or admitted queries miss their deadline. That is Little's law, which relates the number of items in a system to the rate they pass through and the time each spends there: 300 queries a second × 0.2 seconds is 60. A fifth of the one-second timeout leaves the rest for the request's other work and for bursts; allow the whole second, as 300 does, and any other delay misses the deadline. Anywhere between the bounds works; nearer the top absorbs bursts better.</p>

<p>You probably have this limit already, in a form that does not work. A connection pool caps the queries an application runs, but makes a caller wait for a free connection, by default for 30 seconds in HikariCP and SQLAlchemy and for ever in node-postgres, which rebuilds the queue in the application. A pool enforces the bounds when its size, summed over all instances, is what the database runs well at once, and its wait plus the query fits in 0.2 seconds. HikariCP will not wait less than 250 ms, so a semaphore in front of it may have to refuse instead.</p>

<p>Finally, PostgreSQL's <code>statement_timeout</code>, off by default, cancels a query still running after a set time. Set it on the application's role, just above the request's deadline, so a query whose caller has given up is not finished anyway. The model has no such timeout, the worst case.</p>

<h3 id="refused">What the refused requests get</h3>

<p>What refused requests receive is a product decision, better made before an outage than during one. The cheapest answer is an error page asking the user to try again shortly. A product page can drop its recommendations panel, which costs several queries, and still be useful. Some data can be served from a small in-process cache in each application instance, stale but present; <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys">Redis Cluster, sharding and hot keys</ArticleRef> covers these near caches. And requests can be ranked, so that the database's 300 queries a second go to checkout before browsing. The SRE book calls this graceful degradation. Facebook's memcache had a version for caches: when a cache server failed, clients sent its keys to a small standby pool called Gutter, which held them with short TTLs, so those misses did not reach the database.</p>

<p>The same ranking applies to retries. A refused request that the browser or another service retries at once adds to the load the limit is protecting against, so retries need the budgets <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining" hash="retries">Redis clients</ArticleRef> described, at every layer.</p>

<h2 id="warming">Bringing an empty cache into service</h2>

<p>An empty cache is a planned event more often than an accident: a replacement cluster, an upgrade onto fresh servers, a move to a managed service. <ArticleRef slug="stampedes">Cache stampedes</ArticleRef> showed what a cold start does key by key: every popular key misses at once, many times over. This section is about the procedure around it.</p>

<p>The best procedure is to never let the cache be empty. Within Redis, that means a replica. To replace a server, start the new one as a replica of the old, wait for it to synchronise and check that both report the same number of keys. Then allow writes on it (<code>CONFIG SET replica-read-only no</code>), so the application's <code>SET</code>s on a miss are not refused, point the application at it, and promote it with <code>REPLICAOF NO ONE</code> once the old server receives no queries. The new server starts with every key. <ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef> covers how replication works and what it can lose.</p>

<p>Replication is not always available: a managed service may not accept an outside server as its replica, and a move between providers often means the new cache starts empty. Figure 3 compares three ways to put such a cache into service. The old cache is still running and warm; the database has the limit from Figure 2 throughout.</p>

<WarmupFigure number={3} />

<p>Start with switching all traffic at once. The new cache misses on almost everything, the database finishes 300 queries a second, its capacity, for about 15 seconds, and the limit refuses up to 39% of requests in a second, about 2,000 in all. Without the limit this would be Figure 2 again. In the lower chart, the new cache's hit ratio passes 80% about 30 seconds after the switch.</p>

<p>Now choose the ramp. A growing share of requests, starting at 5%, uses the new cache, and the rest stay on the old one. The database's load never comes near its capacity and nothing is refused. The cost shows in the lower chart: the new cache sees little traffic at first, so it takes about 70 seconds to reach 80%. A ramp needs something that sends a chosen share of requests to the new cache, such as a feature flag keyed on a user id.</p>

<p>Finally, try reading the old cache first. Every request uses the new cache, and a miss there reads the old cache before the database, copying what it finds into the new one. The old cache answers most of those misses, so the database barely notices the switch, and the new cache fills as fast as with the switch all at once. Facebook's memcache paper describes the same technique for a new or repaired cluster, and reports that it brought cold clusters to full capacity "in a few hours instead of a few days."</p>

<p>That last option has a cost the model leaves out: the two caches can disagree. When a product changes, the application must delete its key in both, and a read that copies the old cache's value into the new one just after the delete can bring the stale value back, the race <ArticleRef slug="invalidation">Cache invalidation and consistency</ArticleRef> describes. Facebook refused to add a key to the cold cluster for two seconds after a delete. With Redis, delete in both caches, give copied values a short TTL, and stop reading the old cache once the new one's hit ratio has settled, minutes rather than days.</p>

<p>Filling the cache in advance from a list of keys, often the old cache's keys walked with <code>SCAN</code> (never <code>KEYS</code>, as <ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works" hash="scan">How Redis works</ArticleRef> explains) or a sample of recent requests, is the remaining option. It is limited by the database's spare capacity, not by Redis: <em>N</em> keys at <em>S</em> spare queries a second takes <em>N</em> ÷ <em>S</em> seconds, so 100,000 keys at 100 a second takes about 17 minutes. Skewed popularity makes it worthwhile anyway: load the most popular keys first, since they carry most of the reads.</p>

<p>When Redis restarts empty without warning there is no old cache to read and nothing to ramp from. What remains is the limit from Figure 2, which keeps the database answering in time so that its answers refill the cache. One empty start is always self-inflicted: flushing a capacity cache to fix a bad value is a cold start for every key. Delete the bad keys instead, or change the version in their names as <ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values" hash="versions">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef> describes, and know that a new version prefix is itself a cold start, as the next figure shows.</p>

<p>If you must flush, use <code>FLUSHALL ASYNC</code>. Since Redis 7.4 a plain <code>FLUSHALL</code> also frees memory in the background, except inside a transaction or script, where it held every client on my laptop for about 130 ms for 500,000 small keys.</p>

<h2 id="monitoring">Is the cache healthy?</h2>

<p>A cache can fail without going down: it answers every request, quickly, while sending the database far more than it should. So the first things to watch are the database's load from cache misses and the application's latency and errors, which users feel. The cache's own numbers explain them.</p>

<h3 id="hit-ratio">Reading the hit ratio</h3>

<p>Redis counts hits and misses in <code>INFO</code>, as <code>keyspace_hits</code> and <code>keyspace_misses</code>, and the hit ratio is hits ÷ (hits + misses). Three details decide whether that number means anything.</p>

<p>First, both are counters that only grow, from the moment the server started or from the last <code>CONFIG RESETSTAT</code>; on my server both commands set them back to zero. Divide the totals and you get a hit ratio since the last restart, perhaps months ago, which barely moves when something goes wrong today. Divide the differences between two readings a minute apart instead; monitoring systems do this with a rate function.</p>

<p>Second, as <ArticleRef slug="">the introduction</ArticleRef> pointed out, Redis counts lookups by any command, not cache answers. On my server an <code>MGET</code> of three keys counted one hit and two misses, <code>EXISTS</code> and <code>TTL</code> counted too, and a read of a missing field in an existing hash counted as a hit. A cached "not found" marker (<ArticleRef slug="keys-and-values" hash="negative-caching">Cache keys and cached values</ArticleRef>) is a hit as well, right for the database's load but not what a product manager means by "found". A plain <code>SET</code> counts nothing.</p>

<p>Third, the counters cover every key on the server. If sessions, locks or rate limiters share the server, their reads are mixed in. The most useful hit ratio is one the application counts itself, per key family: products, prices, recommendations.</p>

<p>Figure 4 shows why the window matters, and what to look at next. I ran a cache-aside client against Redis at 1,000 reads a second for seven minutes and read <code>INFO</code> every second; at four minutes, something went wrong. Each choice is a separate recording of a different mistake.</p>

<IncidentsFigure number={4} />

<p>Start with maxmemory cut to 6 MB, as a mistaken configuration change might. The hit ratio over the last 10 seconds falls from 85% to about 78%, and three minutes later the hit ratio since start has moved two points. A dashboard showing only the cumulative number shows nothing. The middle chart shows what happened: evictions, which had been zero, jump and then settle at about 200 a second, and the number of keys halves. Evictions mean the cache is too small for what it is asked to hold.</p>

<p>Now choose the TTL cut to 10 seconds, as a mistyped setting might. For about 15 seconds little changes, because stored keys keep the TTL they were written with. Then the old keys expire one by one and their replacements expire quickly, so the hit ratio slides down for minutes and is still falling at the end. There are no evictions; instead expirations rise and the number of keys falls steadily. That pattern, more expirations and fewer keys with memory to spare, means TTLs are shorter than the keys' useful life. <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction" hash="observing">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> gave this diagnosis: evictions point at size, expirations at TTLs.</p>

<p>Two common incidents fit neither. Choose key names changed, the new version prefix from earlier: misses jump to almost 400 a second and recover within a minute, with no evictions, while the number of keys rises, because the old keys stay until their TTLs end. Now choose FLUSHALL. The hit ratio and the misses follow almost exactly the same path, but the number of keys falls to nearly nothing. A restart without persistence looks the same, and also resets the counters and <code>uptime_in_seconds</code>, which is the quickest way to recognise it. Both are cold starts for every key, and the key count tells them apart.</p>

<p>These recordings had spare database capacity; the shop does not. A fall from 85% to 78% raises misses by half, and whether that is an incident depends on where the database's load ends up, which is why the most important alert watches the database.</p>

<h3 id="alerts">What to alert on</h3>

<p>Alerts that page someone should describe something users notice or soon will; the rest belong on a dashboard or in a ticket. For a capacity cache, this is a reasonable starting set; the fields are from <code>INFO</code> on Redis 8.10, read from every node, and ElastiCache publishes most of them under its own names.</p>

