<p>Redis also records its own slow moments. <code>SLOWLOG GET</code> lists commands that took longer than 10 milliseconds by default, and the latency monitor records spikes from forks, expiry and other sources once you give it a threshold (<code>latency-monitor-threshold</code> is 0, off, by default). <ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works" hash="diagnosis">How Redis works</ArticleRef> showed how to use them; they explain an incident after the fact more often than they raise an alert.</p>

<h3 id="per-node">Watching every node</h3>

<p><code>INFO</code> describes only the server you ask. With replicas or Redis Cluster, collect it from every primary and every replica, and alert on the worst node as well as on the total. A cluster-wide hit ratio, memory figure or eviction rate is an average, and it hides one shard that is full and evicting, or one whose main thread is saturated by a hot key while the others idle. Add <code>CLUSTER INFO</code> from each node: <code>cluster_state</code> should be <code>ok</code> and <code>cluster_slots_fail</code> zero, since a failed slot means some keys have no server. Size memory and throughput for the largest shard rather than the average, with room for a reshard and for a replica taking over a primary's load. When one node stands out, <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys" hash="finding-hot-keys">Redis Cluster, sharding and hot keys</ArticleRef> shows how to find the slot or key responsible.</p>

<h2 id="capacity">How big it should be</h2>

<p>Sizing a cache starts from what it must hold, not from what exists. The catalogue may be millions of products, but the reads that matter fall on a much smaller working set, how much smaller depending on the skew of popularity (<ArticleRef slug="">the introduction</ArticleRef>; <ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef> drew hit ratio against cache size). Turning that into a machine needs two more numbers: what one item costs in memory, and how much of the machine's memory Redis can safely use for data.</p>

<p>The first is easy to measure and easy to guess wrongly, since every key carries overhead beyond its value (<ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works" hash="key-memory">How Redis works</ArticleRef>). For a product cache, I wrote 20,000 keys with 2,000-byte values and a TTL and watched <code>used_memory</code> grow: 2,130 bytes a key, about 6% above the key and value, with my laptop's libc allocator. For small values the overhead is a much larger share, and jemalloc on Linux rounds sizes differently, so measure a sample of your real values on your production build.</p>

<p>The second is where most sizing goes wrong, because <code>maxmemory</code> is not all the memory Redis uses, and not all of it is data:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Client buffers count against <code>maxmemory</code>.</strong> Every connection's input and output buffers come out of the same limit as the data, and by default a normal client's output is not capped. When I let one client pipeline 20,000 <code>GET</code>s without reading the replies, its buffer reached 24 MB and Redis evicted three quarters of a 30 MB cache to make room. Watch <code>mem_clients_normal</code>, and cap it with <code>client-output-buffer-limit normal</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Fragmentation</strong>, memory the allocator holds but Redis is not using, is not counted. It can be large after mass deletions (twice <code>used_memory</code> on my laptop's libc build, while Linux builds with jemalloc return freed pages gradually), so check <code>mem_fragmentation_ratio</code> after a large delete.</li>
  <li><strong>Replica and AOF buffers</strong> are left out on purpose: evicting keys to make room for them would create more replication traffic and evict more keys.</li>
  <li><strong>Copy-on-write</strong> pages of a forked child, during a snapshot or a replica's full synchronisation, can reach twice the normal memory under heavy writes, says the Redis administration guide.</li>
</ul>

<p>Its rule of thumb: if you think you have 10 GB free, set <code>maxmemory</code> to 8 or 9. ElastiCache keeps 25% of each node out of <code>maxmemory</code> by default and advises you not to lower it. Figure 5 follows one node from the memory you pay for to the load it leaves on the database, for a catalogue of two million products read 1,000 times a second, with the same 300-query database as Figure 1.</p>

<SizingFigure number={5} />

<p>Start at 1 GB with 15% held back: 870 MB of <code>maxmemory</code>, less about 20 MB of client buffers, leaves 850 MB for about 418,000 products, a fifth of the catalogue, and because popularity is skewed that is enough for a hit ratio of at most 81% and 186 database queries a second. Now choose the 50% reserve that a server taking snapshots under heavy writes needs, and watch copy-on-write take most of the first row. The same node holds 242,000 products and the database's load rises by a third. Go down to 512 MB with that reserve and the database is over capacity even with the cache warm, in the right-hand range of Figure 1. Go up to 4 GB and 84% of the catalogue fits, enough for 98% of reads to hit. Notice how the returns shrink: going from 1 GB to 2 GB removes about 80 database queries a second, and going from 2 GB to 4 GB removes about 80 more, for twice the extra memory.</p>

<p>Two cautions about the figure. Its hit ratios assume the cache always holds the most popular products, which no eviction policy quite achieves, so expect a few points less. And it sizes for a hit ratio, not for survival: every size in it still leaves a capacity cache, which needs the earlier sections' protection.</p>

<p>Memory usually decides a cache's size, but check two other limits. Redis executes commands on one main thread, whose time per request, mostly network work, sets a server's ceiling (<ArticleRef slug="how-redis-works" hash="event-loop">How Redis works</ArticleRef>); the examples in the Redis benchmark documentation show about 180,000 <code>SET</code>s a second without pipelining and about 1.5 million with a pipeline of 16. Watch the main thread's CPU, not the machine's: an eight-core server can be saturated at 13% CPU (ElastiCache's <code>EngineCPUUtilization</code> reports the thread). When one thread is not enough, the answer is more servers, which <ArticleRef slug="cluster-and-hot-keys">Redis Cluster, sharding and hot keys</ArticleRef> covers.</p>

<p>The other is the network. Bandwidth is reads a second times the size of a reply: the shop's 1,000 reads of 2 KB are 2 MB a second, but a page that reads 50 products, at 1,000 pages a second, is 100 MB a second, 0.8 Gbit/s, enough to fill a 1 Gbit/s link long before Redis is busy. Smaller cloud instances often quote bandwidth as "up to" a figure they sustain only briefly, so check the baseline. Finally, count connections: every application instance's pool, added up, must stay well below <code>maxclients</code> (10,000 by default), as <ArticleRef slug="clients-and-pipelining" hash="connections">Redis clients</ArticleRef> explains.</p>

<h2 id="security">Who can reach it</h2>

<p>Redis expects to be reached only by trusted clients inside a trusted network. Its documentation warns against exposing it to the internet, where a single <code>FLUSHALL</code> from an attacker deletes the whole dataset. A cache is no exception: it can be read as well as emptied. Before Redis 7.0, <code>CONFIG SET</code> could also point snapshots at any directory, a well-known route to taking over the host; 7.0 made <code>dir</code> and <code>dbfilename</code> unchangeable at run time by default (<code>enable-protected-configs no</code>), and my 8.10.3 refused both.</p>

<p>Start with where it listens. My Redis 8.10.3, started without a configuration file, listened on every network interface (<code>bind * -::*</code>) with no password. What kept it safe was <strong>protected mode</strong>, on by default since Redis 3.2, which makes a server with no password and the default <code>bind</code> answer only clients on the same machine. Packages, container images and the source's <code>redis.conf</code> choose differently, so run <code>CONFIG GET bind</code> and <code>CONFIG GET protected-mode</code> on what you deploy, put Redis on a private network, and let only the application's hosts reach its port.</p>

<p>Then give each kind of client its own user. Since Redis 6, access control lists (ACLs) create users with their own passwords, limited to certain keys and commands. The default user, which every connection without a username uses, can do everything and has no password, so disable it, but only once everything that connects has a user of its own, including you, your replicas and Sentinel. Users are not replicated, and those created with <code>ACL SETUSER</code> are lost at the next restart unless saved, so keep them in a file and deploy the same file to the primary and every replica:</p>

<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="ACL file and redis.conf lines for every node"><code>{`# /etc/redis/users.acl, the same file on every node
user admin on >admin-password ~* &* +@all
user replica on >replica-password +psync +replconf +ping
user sentinel on >sentinel-password &__sentinel__:hello +multi +slaveof +ping +exec +subscribe +config|rewrite +role +publish +info +client|setname +client|kill +script|kill
user shop on >shop-password ~app:* +@read +@write +@connection +@transaction +@scripting -@dangerous
user metrics on >metrics-password +info +ping +client|setinfo +slowlog|get +slowlog|len +latency|latest +latency|history
user default off

# redis.conf, on every node
aclfile /etc/redis/users.acl
masteruser replica
masterauth replica-password`}</code></pre>

<p>In each line <code>~</code> gives key patterns, and <code>+</code> and <code>-</code> commands or categories. <code>admin</code> keeps a way in for people. Replicas log in to their primary as <code>replica</code>, configured on every node since any node can become a replica, and each Sentinel names the <code>sentinel</code> user, whose rules come from the ACL documentation, in <code>sentinel auth-user</code> and <code>sentinel auth-pass</code>. I started a primary, a replica and a Sentinel with exactly these files: nothing connected without a user, <code>admin</code> could still run <code>ACL</code> and <code>CONFIG</code>, and after a failover the promoted replica accepted <code>shop</code>. Without <code>masteruser</code> the replica could not synchronise, and a Sentinel without <code>auth-user</code> marked the healthy primary down. <code>aclfile</code> can be set only at start-up, so adding it means a restart; to change a user later, edit the file on every node and run <code>ACL LOAD</code> on each.</p>

<p>The <code>shop</code> user could read, write and delete keys under <code>app:</code>, and run what client libraries and earlier patterns need: <code>PING</code>, <code>CLIENT SETINFO</code>, <code>MULTI</code>, <code>WATCH</code> and <code>EVAL</code> on its own keys. Without <code>+@connection</code> even <code>PING</code> is refused, and pools that check connections with it fail; without <code>+@transaction</code>, so are <code>MULTI</code> and <code>WATCH</code>. Leave out <code>+@scripting</code> if you use no scripts; a cluster client also needs <code>+cluster|slots</code> or <code>+cluster|shards</code>, whichever it uses. <code>SET other:1</code> was refused with <code>NOPERM</code>, and so were <code>KEYS</code>, <code>FLUSHALL</code>, <code>CONFIG GET</code> and <code>INFO</code>, which is in <code>@dangerous</code>. Hence <code>metrics</code>, which can read <code>INFO</code>, the slow log and the latency monitor but cannot reset them or touch a key. Each refusal is recorded in <code>ACL LOG</code>, so test the users with your real client library and metrics exporter and read it after deploying. (<code>ACL GENPASS</code> generates a password.)</p>

<p>One result surprised me. A key pattern restricts commands that name keys, and <code>FLUSHALL</code> names none. A user created with <code>~tenant1:* +@all</code> could not read <code>tenant2:secret</code>, but its <code>FLUSHALL</code> succeeded and emptied the whole database, both tenants' keys included. The documentation advises removing such commands explicitly (<code>-flushall -flushdb</code>); denying <code>@dangerous</code>, as above, removes them too. Nor does a pattern hide key names: <code>shop</code>'s <code>SCAN</code> and <code>RANDOMKEY</code> returned <code>other:secret</code>, though it could not read it, which matters when names contain ids. Prefer ACLs to the older <code>rename-command</code>, now marked deprecated.</p>

<p>Last, encryption. Redis supports TLS (Transport Layer Security, the encryption HTTPS uses) for clients, replicas and the cluster bus, each enabled separately (<code>tls-port</code>, <code>tls-replication</code>, <code>tls-cluster</code>), and <code>port 0</code> turns plain connections off. TLS costs CPU on both sides and lowers one server's throughput, so measure it on your own hardware before turning it on for a busy cache; whether you need it inside a private network is a matter of policy.</p>

<h2 id="services">Running it yourself, or not, and which Redis</h2>

<p>A managed service changes details that matter. I checked Amazon ElastiCache; other providers differ, so treat this as questions to ask.</p>

<p>On ElastiCache, <code>maxmemory</code> is the node's memory minus its 25% reserve: a node advertised with 13.5 GB gets about 10.1 GB. In its default parameter groups the eviction policy is <code>volatile-lru</code>, which evicts only keys with a TTL, so a cache key written without one can never be evicted (<ArticleRef slug="expiration-and-eviction">Cache expiration and eviction</ArticleRef>). When there is not enough free memory to fork for a snapshot or a replica's synchronisation, it switches to a forkless save that uses less memory but, in AWS's words, "can add client latencies." Its failover and timing are its own (<ArticleRef slug="replication-and-persistence">Redis replication, persistence and failover</ArticleRef>). Which commands you may run varies by service; check before relying on <code>CONFIG</code>, <code>CLIENT PAUSE</code> or replication into it.</p>

<p>The other question is which software you run. Redis changed its licence twice, and the result decides what you can get:</p>

<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Redis licences by version, and Valkey">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Versions</th><th>Licence</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Redis 7.2 and earlier</td><td>BSD 3-clause, an open-source licence with few conditions</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Redis 7.4 to 7.8</td><td>Your choice of the Redis Source Available License (RSALv2) or the Server Side Public License (SSPLv1), neither of them an open-source licence</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Redis 8.0 and later</td><td>Your choice of RSALv2, SSPLv1 or the GNU Affero General Public License (AGPLv3), which is an open-source licence</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Valkey (all versions)</td><td>BSD 3-clause; a Linux Foundation project forked from Redis 7.2.4</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>I will not interpret the licences, but three practical consequences follow. First, your organisation may have a policy on the AGPL or the SSPL; if it rules them out, the choice is Valkey or an old Redis. Second, your provider's choice may already have been made: ElastiCache offers Valkey (7.2 up to 9.1 at the time of writing) and Redis only up to 7.1, so on it you cannot run Redis 7.4 or later itself. Third, the two have diverged in ways that touch earlier articles:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Compare-and-delete is <code>DELEX … IFEQ</code> in Redis 8.4 and <code>DELIFEQ</code> in Valkey 9.0, so code written for one fails on the other.</li>
  <li><code>allkeys-lrm</code> and <code>HOTKEYS</code> (Redis 8.6) are not in Valkey 9.0; hash-field expiry, in Redis since 7.4, reached Valkey in 9.0.</li>
  <li>Valkey 8.0 and later free deleted, evicted and flushed values in a background thread by default (<code>lazyfree-*</code> is <code>yes</code>), and keep a 10 MB replication backlog against Redis's 1 MB, so a big eviction or <code>FLUSHALL</code> behaves differently after a migration.</li>
</ul>

<p>For a cache that uses only <code>GET</code>, <code>SET</code> and TTLs, any of them will do, and the deciding questions are this article's: who runs it, how it fails over, and what it lets you measure and configure. List the features you rely on, check your choice has them, and reread the defaults on every upgrade.</p>

<h2 id="decisions">What you can now decide</h2>

<p>Back to the pager's questions. At 1,000 reads a second against a database that answers 300, the shop's cache is a capacity cache. While it is gone the application fails fast behind a circuit breaker, sends the database only what it can answer in time, and gives everything else a degraded page or an honest error. You can put an empty cache into service without an outage, and explain from four counters why the hit ratio fell last night.</p>

<p>More generally, you can now:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Work out the range of traffic in which your system depends on its cache, from <em>C</em> and <em>C</em> ÷ (1 − <em>h</em>), and notice when growth moves you into it.</li>
  <li>Test the dependency deliberately, including a Redis that hangs rather than dies.</li>
  <li>Bound the queries sent to the database between what it can usefully run at once and what Little's law allows, with a pool that refuses quickly and a statement timeout.</li>
  <li>Bring an empty cache into service by replication where you can, otherwise by a ramp or by reading the old cache first, and never by flushing a capacity cache.</li>
  <li>Read the hit ratio over a window, tell evictions, expirations and new key names apart, and alert on the database's load, the hit ratio, evictions, memory, connections and errors, for the worst node as well as the total.</li>
  <li>Size memory from the working set and measured bytes per item, leaving room outside <code>maxmemory</code> and for client buffers inside it, and check the main thread, the network and the connections.</li>
  <li>Keep Redis on a private network, give each client, replica and Sentinel a user with only what it needs, from one ACL file on every node, and know that a key pattern does not stop <code>FLUSHALL</code>.</li>
  <li>Ask a managed service how much memory you really get, what its defaults are and what you may run, and choose between Redis and Valkey on features and licence.</li>
</ul>

<p>The articles so far have each taken one decision at a time. <ArticleRef slug="designing-a-cache">Designing a cache: a worked example</ArticleRef> takes one application from its requirements to a complete cache design, making all of them together.</p>

